# Additions to prjuray-db's segbits_bram.db (the BRAM tile's CLB_IO_CLK
# frames). prjuray-db is the reference and is READ ONLY; these are applied by
# tools/apply_db_corrections.py into the writable overlay at prjuray/database/.
#
# Format: <feature key> add-feature <bit>...
# The applier FAILS if the reference already has the feature, so an upstream
# fix is caught rather than shadowed.
#
# prjuray never solved BRAM port widths, the RSTREGB inverter or the bits a
# BRAM sets just by being used, so a bitstream built from the reference alone
# leaves every BRAM at its power-on width with its clock/control columns off.
#
# Evidence: designs/kv260_bram_char (Vivado 2024.2.2, xck26-sfvc784-2LV-c).
# 84 BRAMs, one per tile in BRAM_X19/X33, each LOC'd and differing from a
# baseline in exactly one parameter; analyze_char.py diffs every tile against
# its baseline (L/U/36 baselines are each built twice and are bit-stable).
# Cross-checked on designs/kv260_bram_spec (four mixed-width BRAMs): every
# unknown bit in those tiles is explained by the features below.
#
#  * IN_USE: four bits in frame 5 per half (05_103/05_125 shared). 05_128 is
#    set only by a RAMB36 (not by two RAMB18s in one tile). Identical in every
#    variant of a given kind, whatever the parameters.
#  * ROW<k>.OUTPUTS_ENABLED: three bits per INT row (1_21+48k, 2_27+48k,
#    3_24+48k), set iff some BRAM output pin leaves through that row. In
#    kv260_bram_spec the rows Vivado sets are exactly the rows our routing
#    takes outputs through (the INT_INTF_L_X19Y<row> LOGIC_OUTS crossings):
#    {0-4}, {0-4} and {0-3}; one output in a row is enough. The RAMB36 at
#    Y160 drives no output from row 4 but has nine of its inputs in that row,
#    and the bits stay clear, so inputs do not set them. (In the char design
#    every output is used, which is why a lone lower/upper RAMB18 there sets
#    rows 0-2 / 2-4.)
#  * Port width: a 3-bit code per half and port, 1=000 2=b0 4=b1 9=b0+b1
#    18=b2 (18 is also the default). A RAMB36 port of width W puts the code
#    for W/2 in both halves; W=1 and W=9 additionally set a per-port flag
#    (READ_A 04_136, WRITE_A 04_127, READ_B 04_135, WRITE_B 04_134).
#  * RSTREGBINV: the IS_RSTREGB_INVERTED variants set exactly 04_84 (L) /
#    04_149 (U); the SDP variants set the same bits.

# --- in-use bits: set whenever the half (or the RAMB36) is placed ---
BRAM.RAMB18E2_L.IN_USE add-feature 05_82 05_85 05_103 05_125
BRAM.RAMB18E2_U.IN_USE add-feature 05_103 05_125 05_169 05_172
BRAM.RAMB36E2.IN_USE add-feature 05_82 05_85 05_103 05_125 05_128 05_169 05_172

# --- output enables, one per INT row of the tile (row k = the INT/INT_INTF
# tile k rows above the BRAM tile's own Y) ---
BRAM.ROW0.OUTPUTS_ENABLED add-feature 01_21 02_27 03_24
BRAM.ROW1.OUTPUTS_ENABLED add-feature 01_69 02_75 03_72
BRAM.ROW2.OUTPUTS_ENABLED add-feature 01_117 02_123 03_120
BRAM.ROW3.OUTPUTS_ENABLED add-feature 01_165 02_171 03_168
BRAM.ROW4.OUTPUTS_ENABLED add-feature 01_213 02_219 03_216

# --- RSTREGB inverter (Vivado also sets it in SDP mode) ---
BRAM.RAMB18E2_L.RSTREGBINV.V1 add-feature 04_84
BRAM.RAMB18E2_U.RSTREGBINV.V1 add-feature 04_149
BRAM.RAMB36E2.RSTREGBINV.V1 add-feature 04_84 04_149

# --- RAMB18E2 port widths. Width 1 has no bits; 36 (SDP) is W18 plus the
# reference READ_WIDTH_A.36 / WRITE_WIDTH_B.36 ---
BRAM.RAMB18E2_L.READ_WIDTH_A.W2 add-feature 05_34
BRAM.RAMB18E2_L.READ_WIDTH_A.W4 add-feature 05_37
BRAM.RAMB18E2_L.READ_WIDTH_A.W9 add-feature 05_34 05_37
BRAM.RAMB18E2_L.READ_WIDTH_A.W18 add-feature 05_40
BRAM.RAMB18E2_L.WRITE_WIDTH_A.W2 add-feature 04_42
BRAM.RAMB18E2_L.WRITE_WIDTH_A.W4 add-feature 04_43
BRAM.RAMB18E2_L.WRITE_WIDTH_A.W9 add-feature 04_42 04_43
BRAM.RAMB18E2_L.WRITE_WIDTH_A.W18 add-feature 04_44
BRAM.RAMB18E2_L.READ_WIDTH_B.W2 add-feature 05_31
BRAM.RAMB18E2_L.READ_WIDTH_B.W4 add-feature 05_28
BRAM.RAMB18E2_L.READ_WIDTH_B.W9 add-feature 05_31 05_28
BRAM.RAMB18E2_L.READ_WIDTH_B.W18 add-feature 05_25
BRAM.RAMB18E2_L.WRITE_WIDTH_B.W2 add-feature 04_45
BRAM.RAMB18E2_L.WRITE_WIDTH_B.W4 add-feature 04_46
BRAM.RAMB18E2_L.WRITE_WIDTH_B.W9 add-feature 04_45 04_46
BRAM.RAMB18E2_L.WRITE_WIDTH_B.W18 add-feature 04_47
BRAM.RAMB18E2_U.READ_WIDTH_A.W2 add-feature 04_221
BRAM.RAMB18E2_U.READ_WIDTH_A.W4 add-feature 04_220
BRAM.RAMB18E2_U.READ_WIDTH_A.W9 add-feature 04_221 04_220
BRAM.RAMB18E2_U.READ_WIDTH_A.W18 add-feature 04_219
BRAM.RAMB18E2_U.WRITE_WIDTH_A.W2 add-feature 04_205
BRAM.RAMB18E2_U.WRITE_WIDTH_A.W4 add-feature 04_204
BRAM.RAMB18E2_U.WRITE_WIDTH_A.W9 add-feature 04_205 04_204
BRAM.RAMB18E2_U.WRITE_WIDTH_A.W18 add-feature 04_202
BRAM.RAMB18E2_U.READ_WIDTH_B.W2 add-feature 04_218
BRAM.RAMB18E2_U.READ_WIDTH_B.W4 add-feature 04_214
BRAM.RAMB18E2_U.READ_WIDTH_B.W9 add-feature 04_218 04_214
BRAM.RAMB18E2_U.READ_WIDTH_B.W18 add-feature 04_211
BRAM.RAMB18E2_U.WRITE_WIDTH_B.W2 add-feature 04_197
BRAM.RAMB18E2_U.WRITE_WIDTH_B.W4 add-feature 04_196
BRAM.RAMB18E2_U.WRITE_WIDTH_B.W9 add-feature 04_197 04_196
BRAM.RAMB18E2_U.WRITE_WIDTH_B.W18 add-feature 04_194

# --- RAMB36E2 port widths: each half carries the 18K code for W/2, and
# widths 1 and 9 add a per-port flag; W2 has no bits. 72 (SDP) is W36 plus both halves'
# READ_WIDTH_A.36 / WRITE_WIDTH_B.36 ---
BRAM.RAMB36E2.READ_WIDTH_A.W1 add-feature 04_136
BRAM.RAMB36E2.READ_WIDTH_A.W4 add-feature 04_221 05_34
BRAM.RAMB36E2.READ_WIDTH_A.W9 add-feature 04_136 04_220 05_37
BRAM.RAMB36E2.READ_WIDTH_A.W18 add-feature 04_220 04_221 05_34 05_37
BRAM.RAMB36E2.READ_WIDTH_A.W36 add-feature 04_219 05_40
BRAM.RAMB36E2.WRITE_WIDTH_A.W1 add-feature 04_127
BRAM.RAMB36E2.WRITE_WIDTH_A.W4 add-feature 04_42 04_205
BRAM.RAMB36E2.WRITE_WIDTH_A.W9 add-feature 04_43 04_127 04_204
BRAM.RAMB36E2.WRITE_WIDTH_A.W18 add-feature 04_42 04_43 04_204 04_205
BRAM.RAMB36E2.WRITE_WIDTH_A.W36 add-feature 04_44 04_202
BRAM.RAMB36E2.READ_WIDTH_B.W1 add-feature 04_135
BRAM.RAMB36E2.READ_WIDTH_B.W4 add-feature 04_218 05_31
BRAM.RAMB36E2.READ_WIDTH_B.W9 add-feature 04_135 04_214 05_28
BRAM.RAMB36E2.READ_WIDTH_B.W18 add-feature 04_214 04_218 05_28 05_31
BRAM.RAMB36E2.READ_WIDTH_B.W36 add-feature 04_211 05_25
BRAM.RAMB36E2.WRITE_WIDTH_B.W1 add-feature 04_134
BRAM.RAMB36E2.WRITE_WIDTH_B.W4 add-feature 04_45 04_197
BRAM.RAMB36E2.WRITE_WIDTH_B.W9 add-feature 04_46 04_134 04_196
BRAM.RAMB36E2.WRITE_WIDTH_B.W18 add-feature 04_45 04_46 04_196 04_197
BRAM.RAMB36E2.WRITE_WIDTH_B.W36 add-feature 04_47 04_194

# --- Simple dual port, renamed. prjuray names the SDP bits READ_WIDTH_A.36 /
# WRITE_WIDTH_B.36 (RAMB36E2: .72), but a FASM feature segment cannot start
# with a digit, so nothing can write those names (the textx parser stops at
# them: a 512x32 FIFO, yosys's RAMB18E2 SDP, in designs/kv260_tokenizer_sm).
# Same bits, named SDP; nextpnr writes <port>.W<full> plus <port>.SDP. ---
BRAM.RAMB18E2_L.READ_WIDTH_A.SDP add-feature 05_49
BRAM.RAMB18E2_L.WRITE_WIDTH_B.SDP add-feature 05_64
BRAM.RAMB18E2_U.READ_WIDTH_A.SDP add-feature 05_208
BRAM.RAMB18E2_U.WRITE_WIDTH_B.SDP add-feature 05_214
BRAM.RAMB36E2.READ_WIDTH_A.SDP add-feature 05_49 05_208
BRAM.RAMB36E2.WRITE_WIDTH_B.SDP add-feature 05_64 05_214
